<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>How the guide pipeline works</title>
<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View><w:Zoom>100</w:Zoom></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]-->
<style>
  @page { size: A4; margin: 2.2cm; }
  body { font-family: "Mulish", "Segoe UI", sans-serif; color: #000; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.55; }
  h1 { font-size: 20pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 4pt 0; }
  h2 { font-size: 15pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 18pt 0 6pt 0; }
  h3 { font-size: 12.5pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 14pt 0 4pt 0; }
  p, li { font-size: 11pt; }
  pre, code { font-family: "Roboto Mono", "Roboto", Consolas, monospace; font-size: 9.5pt; color: #000; }
  pre { border: 1pt solid #000; padding: 8pt 10pt; margin: 6pt 0 10pt 0; white-space: pre-wrap; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 6pt 0 10pt 0; width: 100%; }
  th, td { border: 1pt solid #000; padding: 4pt 8pt; font-size: 10.5pt; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-weight: bold; }
  hr { border: none; border-top: 1pt solid #000; margin: 14pt 0; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>How the guide pipeline works</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A short internal note in the <code>satva-doc</code> house style: all black, Mulish, no colour.</p>
  <hr>

  <h2>1. What is it, in simple words?</h2>
  <p>The <b>guide pipeline</b> turns a running app into a finished guide. It takes screenshots, films a short
  walkthrough, and prints everything into a branded PDF. You run one command and get all three files.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it makes</th><th>Script</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Screenshots</td><td>PNG files with numbered rings</td><td><code>callouts.mjs</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Walkthrough</td><td>Animated GIF and a storyboard</td><td><code>cursor-demo.mjs</code>, <code>build-gif.py</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Guide</td><td>A4 PDF with banner, footer and cover</td><td><code>build-pdf.mjs</code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>2. What this note covers</h2>
  <p>Where each part lives, and the one command that runs them all. It is an <b>internal</b> note. A guide that
  goes to a client is built with <code>satva-guide-gif</code>, because that one carries the Satva banner.</p>

  <h2>3. Step-by-step flow</h2>
  <h3>Step 1: copy the starter</h3>
  <pre>cp -r ~/.claude/skills/satva-guide-gif/starter my-guide
cd my-guide &amp;&amp; npm install</pre>
  <h3>Step 2: run it</h3>
  <pre>npm run build</pre>
  <p>This runs <code>build.mjs</code>, which calls the four stages in order and stops at the first failure.</p>

  <h2>4. How it works end to end</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Action</th><th>Layer</th><th>What guards it</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Ring an element</td><td><code>callouts.mjs</code></td><td><code>report()</code> lists every selector that missed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Encode the GIF</td><td><code>build-gif.py</code></td><td>Size ladder, then a check that the file is animated and under 12 MB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Print the PDF</td><td><code>build-pdf.mjs</code></td><td>Uses Chrome if present, otherwise the bundled Chromium</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>5. What this gave us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>One command</b> instead of four hand-run scripts.</li>
    <li><b>Same look every time</b>: header, footer, fonts and ring style never drift.</li>
    <li><b>No secrets in pictures</b>: the pipeline runs on fake demo data by design.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Where everything lives</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>skills/satva-guide-gif/scripts/</code></td><td>The four pipeline scripts</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>skills/satva-guide-gif/assets/</code></td><td>Banner, footer bar and fonts</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>skills/satva-guide-gif/starter/</code></td><td>A working project to copy</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>examples/setup-guide-sample/</code></td><td>The finished PDF, GIF and screenshots</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p><b>In one sentence:</b> one command turns a running app into a branded PDF guide with a narrated GIF.</p>
</body>
</html>
